CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: Paul gives the believer commands where he commands and limits where he does not, and the limits are not one rule restated. It has tried to show that from his sentences. Where Paul commands, there is no liberty to weigh: fornication and idolatry are to be fled, and the works of the flesh are named with a warning. Where he leaves a matter open, meats and drink and days, he tells the believer to be fully persuaded in his own mind and tells each party how not to treat the other. The one who eats is not to despise, the one who does not eat is not to judge, and God has received both."),
("p", "He then gives limits, and each has its own person and ground. The strong are not to put a stumblingblock in a brother’s way, because Christ died for him. They are to bear the infirmities of the weak, because Christ pleased not himself. In Corinth they are not to let their liberty embolden a weak brother to eat against his conscience. They are not to sit at the table of devils, because the Lord’s table and that table exclude each other. They are to eat what is sold and what is set before them, asking no question, until someone says it was offered, and then they are to eat not for his sake. They are not to use liberty for an occasion to the flesh, and they are not to let any man judge them in meat, in drink or in days. The sentence “do all to the glory of God” sums up the course. It does not replace the steps."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What does Paul command?", "Flee fornication and idolatry; the works of the flesh carry a warning", "1Cor 6:18; 10:14; Gal 5:19–21"),
    ("What does he leave open?", "Meats, drink and days: be fully persuaded in your own mind", "Rom 14:2, 5, 6"),
    ("What is forbidden to both parties?", "The eater not to despise; the abstainer not to judge", "Rom 14:3"),
    ("What is the first limit on the strong?", "No stumblingblock to a brother for whom Christ died", "Rom 14:13–15; 1Cor 8:9–13"),
    ("What is the second?", "No fellowship with devils; not the Lord’s table and the table of devils", "1Cor 10:14–22"),
    ("What is the third?", "Another man’s conscience, when he says it was offered", "1Cor 10:28, 29"),
    ("What is liberty not?", "An occasion to the flesh; a yoke; a rule laid by men", "Gal 5:1, 13; Col 2:16–22"),
    ("What is conscience?", "An inner witness to a man’s own conduct; weak, good, defiled or seared", "1Cor 8; 1Tim 1:5; 4:2"),
    ("Is there one rule?", "No: the limits have different persons and grounds", "Chapter 15"),
    ("What does Paul leave unsaid?", "Much that churches have supplied", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.30, 0.46, 0.24]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says. In either case the reader will have read the passages in their places."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. Constable, Piper, Lee and Baidoo each read parts of these passages carefully, and each sees things the volume has used. Where one of them adds a method or an inference the sentences do not state, or draws a line the volume does not draw, the volume has said so and has answered from the text. If the volume has been unfair, the verses will show it."),
]

